    {
      "@id": "references/10665-311941",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Book"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "WHO guideline: recommendations on digital interventions for health system strengthening",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2019-04-16",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "bibo:isbn": "9789241550505",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/311941",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "This WHO guideline provides evidence-based recommendations on the use of digital interventions to strengthen health systems and support progress towards universal health coverage (UHC). Developed in response to the growing adoption of digital technologies and the 2018 World Health Assembly resolution on digital health, it addresses the need for rigorous evaluation of digital health interventions to ensure that investments are informed by evidence of their benefits, harms, acceptability, feasibility, resource implications and equity considerations. The guideline emphasizes that digital technologies should complement, rather than replace, the fundamental components of well-functioning health systems.\n\nThe recommendations focus on a prioritized set of digital health interventions accessible at a minimum through mobile devices, including birth and death notification, stock notification and commodity management, client-to-provider and provider-to-provider telemedicine, targeted client communication, digital tracking of clients’ health status and services, health worker decision support, and digital learning for health workers. The guideline also outlines implementation considerations, identifies research gaps, and supports ministries of health, public health practitioners, development partners and other stakeholders in making evidence-based decisions on the adoption, scale-up and institutionalization of effective digital health interventions.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/10665-311941.dc.json"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/10665-329964",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Report"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "WHO recommends social network-based HIV testing approaches for key populations as part of partner services package: policy brief",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2019-11-22",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/329964",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/10665-329964.dc.json"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/9789241550581-eng",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Book"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Consolidated guidelines on HIV testing services, 2019",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2020-11-03",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "bibo:isbn": "9789241550581 (electronic version)",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/336323",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "This guideline provides comprehensive, evidence-based recommendations to support countries in delivering high-quality, person-centred HIV testing services (HTS) across diverse populations and settings. Updating and consolidating previous WHO guidance issued in 2015 and 2016, it incorporates new evidence on HIV self-testing, social network-based HIV testing approaches, western blotting, and strategic service delivery to strengthen progress towards global HIV targets. Developed using the GRADE methodology and informed by systematic reviews, modelling studies and expert consultation, the guideline aims to improve the effectiveness, efficiency and quality of HIV testing while promoting timely linkage to prevention, treatment and care.\n\nThe publication addresses the full continuum of HIV testing services, including demand creation, pre- and post-test services, facility- and community-based testing, implementation considerations for priority populations, strategic planning, diagnostic testing strategies, quality assurance and programme monitoring. It provides updated recommendations on HIV self-testing, provider-assisted referral, social network-based approaches and the use of dual HIV/syphilis rapid diagnostic tests, while emphasizing the WHO 5Cs of HIV testing services: consent, confidentiality, counselling, correct results and connection. Intended for policy-makers, national programme managers, health-care providers and implementing partners, the guideline supports the expansion of equitable, evidence-informed HIV testing services to improve diagnosis and strengthen national HIV responses.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/9789241550581-eng.dc.json"
    },
    {
