    {
      "@id": "references/10665-331170",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Book"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "WHO consolidated guidelines on tuberculosis: module 1: prevention: tuberculosis preventive treatment",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2020-02-25",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "bibo:isbn": "9789240001503",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/331170",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "This guideline provides updated WHO recommendations for the programmatic management of tuberculosis preventive treatment (TPT) as part of the WHO consolidated guidelines on tuberculosis. It focuses on the prevention of active tuberculosis among people at increased risk of disease progression, particularly people living with HIV, household contacts of patients with tuberculosis and other vulnerable population groups. The document incorporates new evidence on shorter rifamycin-containing preventive treatment regimens and updates previous WHO guidance to improve the clarity, consistency and global applicability of recommendations.\n\nDeveloped in accordance with WHO Guideline Review Committee requirements, the publication presents 18 evidence-based recommendations covering the identification of populations eligible for preventive treatment, exclusion of active tuberculosis, testing for latent tuberculosis infection, recommended preventive treatment regimens, monitoring and evaluation, and implementation considerations. Recommendations were formulated using the GRADE approach, taking into account the certainty of evidence, effectiveness, safety, values and preferences, equity, resource implications, acceptability and feasibility. The guideline is intended to support national policy-makers, programme managers and health-care providers in strengthening tuberculosis prevention and advancing implementation of the WHO End TB Strategy.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/10665-331170.dc.json"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/10665-331525",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Book"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "WHO operational handbook on tuberculosis: module 1: prevention: tuberculosis preventive treatment",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2020-03-23",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "bibo:isbn": "9789240002906",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/331525",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "This operational handbook provides practical guidance for the programmatic management and scale-up of tuberculosis preventive treatment (TPT) in line with WHO consolidated guidelines on tuberculosis. The publication addresses the global burden of tuberculosis infection and the need to expand preventive interventions among populations at high risk of developing TB disease, particularly people living with HIV, household contacts of TB patients and other vulnerable groups. It supports implementation of commitments made at the United Nations High-Level Meeting on Tuberculosis and outlines strategies to strengthen national responses through integrated, people-centred approaches.\n\nThe handbook describes the cascade of care for TPT, including identification of target populations, exclusion of active TB disease, testing for TB infection, treatment options, management of adverse events, adherence support, monitoring and evaluation, and ethical considerations. It also provides operational guidance for contact investigation, integration of preventive services with active TB case finding and HIV services, health system strengthening, and resource mobilization. Practical implementation tools, policy considerations and monitoring indicators are included to assist national TB programmes, policy-makers and health-care providers in adapting and scaling up TPT services in diverse epidemiological and health system settings.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/10665-331525.dc.json"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/10665-350214",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Book"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Comprehensive package of care for infants and young children exposed to HIV: policy brief",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2021-12-09",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "bibo:isbn": "9789240040236 (electronic version)",
