    {
      "@id": "references/10665-349550",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Book"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Global guidance on criteria and processes for validation: elimination of mother-to-child transmission of HIV, syphilis and hepatitis B virus",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2021-11-25",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "bibo:isbn": "9789240039360 (electronic version)",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/349550",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "This guidance document provides updated World Health Organization criteria and processes for validating the elimination of mother-to-child transmission (EMTCT) of HIV, syphilis and hepatitis B virus (HBV) as public health priorities. Building on previous editions focused on HIV and syphilis, this third version incorporates validation guidance for HBV within the framework of the Triple Elimination Initiative. The document outlines standardized indicators, targets and assessment procedures for evaluating progress towards EMTCT across diverse epidemiological and programmatic settings. It addresses impact and process indicators, surveillance and laboratory quality standards, programme assessment, and requirements related to human rights, gender equality and community engagement.\n\nThe document emphasizes integrated maternal and child health approaches, universal access to quality antenatal and prevention services, and the importance of strong health systems and surveillance mechanisms. It also provides guidance for countries with high disease burdens through the Path to Elimination framework, as well as for countries with small numbers of affected pregnant women and special populations. Intended for national, regional and global validation bodies, the document supports harmonized validation processes while allowing countries to pursue single, dual or triple EMTCT validation according to national readiness and regional priorities.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/10665-349550.dc.json"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/10665-344249",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Book"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Global strategy on digital health 2020-2025",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2021-08-18",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "bibo:isbn": "9789240020924 (electronic version)",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/344249",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "The Global strategy on digital health 2020–2025 provides a framework to support Member States in strengthening health systems through the appropriate adoption and use of digital technologies. Developed in response to World Health Assembly mandates and aligned with the Sustainable Development Goals and WHO’s Triple Billion targets, the strategy outlines a shared vision for improving health and well-being through accessible, affordable, secure and sustainable digital health solutions. It emphasizes the importance of strong governance, interoperability, ethical use of data, privacy protection, workforce capacity-building and investment in digital infrastructure to support national health priorities and universal health coverage.\n\nThe document establishes four strategic objectives: promoting global collaboration and knowledge exchange; advancing the implementation of national digital health strategies; strengthening governance for digital health at global, regional and national levels; and advocating people-centred health systems enabled by digital technologies. It also presents guiding principles, an implementation framework and monitoring mechanisms to support countries in developing interoperable digital health ecosystems. Particular attention is given to addressing barriers faced by low- and middle-income countries and ensuring that digital transformation contributes to equitable, effective and resilient health systems.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/10665-344249.dc.json"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/10665-340190",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Book"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Guidelines: updated recommendations on HIV prevention, infant diagnosis, antiretroviral initiation and monitoring",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2021-03-17",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "bibo:isbn": "9789240022232 (electronic version)",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/340190",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "This guideline presents updated WHO recommendations on selected priority areas across the HIV prevention, diagnosis and treatment continuum, developed in response to emerging evidence, implementation experience and evolving clinical practice. Prepared according to WHO Guideline Review Committee procedures and informed by systematic reviews using the GRADE approach, it updates and complements the 2016 consolidated HIV guidelines. The publication addresses key public health challenges affecting the global HIV response, particularly in resource-limited settings, and supports progress towards ending AIDS as a public health threat by 2030 through a public health approach to service delivery.\n\nThe guideline introduces or updates recommendations on the use of the dapivirine vaginal ring as an additional HIV prevention option for women at substantial risk of infection, point-of-care technologies for infant HIV diagnosis and viral load monitoring, revised treatment monitoring algorithms, and earlier initiation of antiretroviral therapy for people living with HIV and tuberculosis. It also provides updated clinical considerations for rapid antiretroviral therapy initiation and outlines implementation principles to support national adaptation. Intended for policy-makers, programme managers, clinicians and implementing partners, the guideline aims to strengthen evidence-based HIV prevention, diagnosis, treatment monitoring and care across diverse settings.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/10665-340190.dc.json"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/9789240016514-eng",
      "@type": [
