    {
      "@id": "references/10665-59577",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Document"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Guidelines for the prevention and control of infection with LAV/HTLV III",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization. Control Programme on AIDS"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "1986-12-31",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/59577",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "These guidelines provide recommendations for preventing and controlling infection with LAV/HTLV-III, the retrovirus recognized at the time as the etiological agent of acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS). Developed as part of the WHO AIDS Control Programme, they are intended primarily for public health authorities and health professionals, who are responsible for adapting the guidance to different populations and settings. The document summarizes available knowledge on transmission, clinical manifestations, laboratory detection, surveillance, notification and confidentiality, and case definitions for AIDS.\n\nThe guidelines emphasize routine precautions to prevent exposure to blood and other potentially infectious body fluids, particularly among health care workers, laboratory personnel and providers of home and emergency care. They address prevention and management of needlestick, parenteral and mucous-membrane exposures, safe handling of specimens and contaminated equipment, and precautions for non-health-care workers. Guidance is also provided on disinfection, sterilization, and the safe processing of reusable needles, syringes and other instruments.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/10665-59577.dc.json"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/10665-107919",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Report"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "8th National Tuberculosis Programme Managers Meeting and 13th Wolfheze Workshop: Wolfheze 2008: tuberculosis management, surveillance and evaluation in Europe with high rates or threat of multidrug resistant tuberculosis: The Hague, the Netherlands, 1–2 June 2008",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization. Regional Office for Europe"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2008-12-31",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization. Regional Office for Europe",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/107919",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "The WHO European Region has the highest rates in the world of drug-resistant tuberculosis and increasing rates of HIV/tuberculosis coinfection. Eighteen high-priority countries have been identified to urgently scale up tuberculosis control interventions: Armenia, Azerbaijan, Belarus, Bulgaria, Estonia, Georgia, Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Latvia, Lithuania, the Republic of Moldova, Romania, the Russian Federation, Tajikistan, Turkey, Turkmenistan, Ukraine and Uzbekistan. Five of these countries are members of the European Union. Migration and pockets of poverty increase vulnerability to tuberculosis in many western European countries. Related areas, such as the rapid detection of drug-resistant tuberculosis, contact tracing, infection control, health system strengthening and the review of national tuberculosis programmes, are of equal concern among eastern, central and western countries of the WHO European Region. While general awareness and political commitment were raised during the Ministerial Forum held in Berlin in October 2007, how to ensure the follow-up of the Berlin Declaration on TB and monitor its implementation is currently under discussion. The 8th National Tuberculosis Programme Managers Meeting and the 13th Wolfheze Workshop, jointly organized in June 2008 in The Hague, provided a forum for discussion on each of the aforementioned topics and facilitated the sharing of experiences between countries in the Region – which are very different from an epidemiological, organizational, cultural and wealth perspective.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/10665-107919.dc.json"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/10665-351406",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Report"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "The case for investing in public health: the strengthening public health services and capacity. A key pillar of the European regional health policy framework Health 2020",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization. Regional Office for Europe"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2014-12-31",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization. Regional Office for Europe",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/351406",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "The economic crisis has led to increased demand and reduced resources for health sectors. The trend for increasing healthcare costs to individuals, the health sector and wider society is significant. Public health can be part of the solution to this challenge. The evidence shows that prevention can be cost-effective, provide value for money andgive returns on investment in both the short and longer terms. This public health summary outlines quick returns on investment for health and other sectors for interventions that promote physical activity and healthy employment; address housing and mental health; and reduce road traffic injuries and violence. Vaccinations and screening programmes are largely cost-effective. Population-level approaches are estimated to cost on average five times less than individual interventions. This report gives examples of interventions with early returns on investment and approaches with longer-term gains. Investing in cost-effective interventions to reduce costs to the health sector and other sectors can help create sustainable health systems and economies for the future.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/10665-351406.dc.json"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/10665-346431",
